// Package remote declares the primitive the query lowering emits in place of a
// `#[query]` function's body. It is the intrinsic half of sngl:remote, which
// declares the box a program reads.
//
// One read-triggered lookup rather than a settle plus a kicker. The first design
// keyed one box to the declaration and fired a kicker from each reactive
// dependency's setter, which cannot express a key that is not a reactive var —
// and per-call-site keys routinely are not. A loop variable has no setter, so
// there is nothing for a kicker to watch:
//
//     for p = pages { row(users = users(p).or([])) }
//
// Reading instead is what makes that work. A prop mentioning `p` is
// re-evaluated by the ordinary dependency walk, the re-evaluation looks up a
// different key, and a key with no fetch behind it starts one. Nothing about the
// query has to be known in advance.
//
// The declaration is a signature: the answer comes from the target's native
// implementation of the marked id, so a backend that does not implement one has
// nothing to fall back to.

import . "sngl:internal/marks"
import remote "sngl:remote"

// Returns the box for this query and key, starting the fetch if no attempt has
// been made for it.
//
// `queryID` names the declaration, `args` is the argument tuple that keys the
// box within it, and `fetch` produces the answer. Generic in T rather than
// erased to `dyn`: the lowering knows the fetched type and substitutes it, so a
// backend sees `func() list<User>` and can emit a typed generic instead of `any`
// and a cast at every use.
//
// `readonly`, not `mutates`. It depends on host state and must never fold at
// build time, but a second call with the same key returns the same box and
// starts nothing — which is the only footing on which a call sitting in a prop
// expression, evaluated during render, is acceptable at all.
#[intrinsic("RemoteQuery", readonly)]
func RemoteQuery<T>(queryID string, args list<dyn>, fetch func() T) remote.Value<T>
